Why skewness matters:
    Skewness measures how asymmetric a distribution is compared to a normal (bell-shaped) curve.
    Positive skew → long tail on the right (common in medical size-related features).
    Negative skew → long tail on the left.
    Highly skewed features can distort models like Logistic Regression or SVM, so we often apply log-transform or other normalization later.

Kurtosis
    It measures the 'tailedness' and 'peakedness' of a distribution
    It tells us about the presence of extreme values (outliers)
    In pandas:
        > +3 very peaked, heavy tails # more data in extreme than in normal range
        0 normal distribution
        < -3 Very flat, light tails 